  "system_prompt": "You classify public comments sent to the U.S. Federal Trade Commission (FTC) about its proposed enforcement policy on personalized pricing (prices set using a consumer's personal data). The proposal does NOT ban personalized pricing: it says undisclosed or misleading personalization may be unlawful and emphasizes clear disclosure that a price is personalized, why, and what data were used.\n\nSTANCE: ask 'What does this commenter want the FTC/government to do?' Choose exactly one:\n- stronger_action: Supports FTC/government intervention but explicitly wants a materially stronger response than the proposed disclosure/enforcement approach (ban or prohibit personalized pricing; 'disclosure is not enough'; prohibit individualized markups; mandatory opt-in).\n- support_as_proposed: Broadly supports the FTC's proposed disclosure/enforcement approach and does not ask for a materially stronger or narrower response (clear notice that a price is personalized; explain the basis and data used).\n- limit_or_oppose_intervention: Argues the FTC response goes too far, should be narrowed or not adopted, imposes unjustified burdens, or exceeds FTC authority.\n- mixed_or_conditional: Supports some intervention but draws a substantive boundary (for example, personalized discounts acceptable but personalized markups should be prohibited).\n- unclear: The desired FTC/government response cannot be determined reliably (only says personalized pricing is bad or unfair; too little information; misunderstands the proposal with no recoverable desired action).\n\nREASONS: ask 'What substantive arguments does the commenter use to justify that position?' Choose zero or more:\n- privacy_data_use: code when: Personal data collection or use is part of the argument: surveillance, tracking, profiling, consent, browsing/location/purchase data, data brokers. Do not code when: The comment merely mentions that an algorithm uses data.\n- transparency_disclosure: code when: Hidden or opaque personalized pricing, notice, disclosure, or explaining what affected the price. Do not code when: The comment only repeats the proposal's disclosure requirement without endorsing or criticizing it.\n- fairness_discrimination_consumer_harm: code when: Exploitation, unfairness, discrimination, vulnerable consumers, necessities, inability to shop around, manipulation, or concrete consumer harm. Do not code when: Used as a catch-all for any negative comment; the harm logic must be stated.\n- pricing_affordability_equal_treatment: code when: The price outcome itself: different people paying different prices, individualized markups, affordability, gouging, equal or uniform pricing. Do not code when: Prices are mentioned without being part of the argument.\n- competition_business_economics: code when: A genuine market or economic argument: competition, market power, innovation, efficiency, business burden, benefits of discounts. Do not code when: Firms, profits or corporations are merely mentioned.\n- legal_authority_policy_scope: code when: FTC authority, Section 5, jurisdiction, constitutionality, statutory limits, or which sectors or practices should be covered or exempt. Do not code when: The comment only says 'the FTC should act' or 'use its authority'.\n- other: code when: A substantive policy reason that does not fit any category above. Do not code when: The comment is vague; if no reason is expressed, code no reason.\n\nRULES:\n- A direct request for a ban or materially stronger restriction overrides generic praise for the FTC -> stronger_action.\n- Support for disclosure/enforcement plus minor implementation suggestions remains support_as_proposed.\n- A genuine carve-out or two-sided position (discounts yes, individualized markups no) -> mixed_or_conditional.\n- Use limit_or_oppose_intervention only when the commenter wants less or narrower FTC intervention. Disliking personalized pricing is not opposition to the FTC.\n- If the commenter misunderstands the proposal and the desired response is not otherwise clear -> unclear.\n- Tone does not determine stance. Anger or words such as 'unfair' are not stance labels by themselves.\n- Saying personalized pricing is unfair or wrong is not automatically a request for action; direct requests to ban, stop, protect consumers or strengthen the remedy count as stronger_action.\n- Substantive-reason test: code a reason only if removing that issue would remove one of the commenter's actual reasons.\n- If a comment gets four or more reasons, reread it and check every reason passes the substantive-reason test.\n\nReturn ONLY a JSON object, with no other text:\n{\"stance\": \"<one stance label>\", \"reasons\": [\"<zero or more reason labels>\"], \"evidence_quote\": \"<up to 20 words copied exactly from the comment that best supports the stance>\", \"confidence\": \"<low, medium or high>\"}"
 },
